WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:09.015
&gt;&gt; Tanuja is a
co-founder of DataGlen,

00:00:09.016 --> 00:00:11.250
which is a early,

00:00:11.251 --> 00:00:14.880
around startup, in
this space of solar energy.

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:16.950
Tanuja was

00:00:16.951 --> 00:00:19.605
earlier a technical staff member
at IBM Research,

00:00:19.606 --> 00:00:22.970
She finished a masters from MIT.

00:00:22.971 --> 00:00:24.900
It's a pleasure to have
you, and she's also part of

00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:27.270
the summer workshop
that we have here,

00:00:27.271 --> 00:00:30.705
so she'll be around for
next couple of weeks.

00:00:30.706 --> 00:00:34.460
&gt;&gt; Thanks Patrick.
Good morning, everyone.

00:00:34.461 --> 00:00:37.950
It's great to present some of
the work that we're doing,

00:00:37.951 --> 00:00:39.750
and I think it's very relevant to

00:00:39.751 --> 00:00:41.130
Azure Analytics part that

00:00:41.131 --> 00:00:42.900
we are talking as
part of the workshop.

00:00:42.901 --> 00:00:44.910
So, as the name suggests,

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:47.220
I'm going to talk
about Bits and Joules.

00:00:47.221 --> 00:00:49.710
So, some of the use
cases in energy

00:00:49.711 --> 00:00:52.450
vertical and what
are the challenges?

00:00:52.451 --> 00:00:54.505
How the paradigm is shifting?

00:00:54.506 --> 00:00:57.270
How the modern day's technologies

00:00:57.271 --> 00:00:59.220
or digital technologies
of IoT and

00:00:59.221 --> 00:01:01.290
AI are relevant for
some of the problems

00:01:01.291 --> 00:01:04.690
that you're trying to
solve in energy particle?

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.810
So, as all of us would
agree that energy is

00:01:09.811 --> 00:01:13.475
a fundamental necessity
of modern society,

00:01:13.476 --> 00:01:16.260
and as we have the access
to clean and cheap energy,

00:01:16.261 --> 00:01:20.010
we can do a lot in terms
of the other sectors,

00:01:20.011 --> 00:01:22.140
including the healthcare and

00:01:22.141 --> 00:01:23.820
education and various
other factors.

00:01:23.821 --> 00:01:25.425
So, it's important to

00:01:25.426 --> 00:01:27.210
basically look at the
problem of energy,

00:01:27.211 --> 00:01:28.905
which is the driving factor for

00:01:28.906 --> 00:01:31.430
various other industries
and technologies,

00:01:31.431 --> 00:01:33.180
in terms of the innovations.

00:01:33.181 --> 00:01:34.800
Energy vertical right now is

00:01:34.801 --> 00:01:36.495
going through a paradigm shift,

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:39.920
and we call that paradigm
shift as Democratization.

00:01:39.921 --> 00:01:41.390
So, on the left-hand side,

00:01:41.391 --> 00:01:43.935
you would see the
democratization of media.

00:01:43.936 --> 00:01:47.710
So, few years back or let say 20,

00:01:47.711 --> 00:01:50.550
30 years back, the media
was pretty centralized,

00:01:50.551 --> 00:01:51.890
so there was content which was

00:01:51.891 --> 00:01:53.790
created centralized way and

00:01:53.791 --> 00:01:55.440
the content was basically

00:01:55.441 --> 00:01:58.330
disseminated in
a distributed manner.

00:01:58.331 --> 00:02:00.070
So, it's like
through national BBC

00:02:00.071 --> 00:02:01.430
who's creating the content,

00:02:01.431 --> 00:02:04.125
and the mass population

00:02:04.126 --> 00:02:06.470
is basically just
absorbing the content.

00:02:06.471 --> 00:02:08.895
But now in last 10 years,

00:02:08.896 --> 00:02:12.550
there is with the social media
like Facebook and YouTube,

00:02:12.551 --> 00:02:14.235
everyone can create the content

00:02:14.236 --> 00:02:16.960
and everyone can consume
that content as well,

00:02:16.961 --> 00:02:19.290
so it's completely changing from

00:02:19.291 --> 00:02:21.710
the centralized to
decentralized manner,

00:02:21.711 --> 00:02:23.310
very similar thing
which is happening

00:02:23.311 --> 00:02:24.690
in the energy vertical right now,

00:02:24.691 --> 00:02:26.745
which is democratization
of energy.

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:28.635
So, previously, there was

00:02:28.636 --> 00:02:30.870
a central generation which was

00:02:30.871 --> 00:02:33.790
happening at the form of
the hydro or thermal energy.

00:02:33.791 --> 00:02:36.000
It was, the distribution
and transmission was

00:02:36.001 --> 00:02:38.170
happening through
central entities.

00:02:38.171 --> 00:02:40.410
Again, in India, most of
the utilities are doing

00:02:40.411 --> 00:02:43.110
the transmission and
distribution right now,

00:02:43.111 --> 00:02:44.510
and the consumers who are

00:02:44.511 --> 00:02:46.580
basically the residential or
industrial consumers,

00:02:46.581 --> 00:02:48.200
are just consuming
the energy without

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:50.815
participating in
the entire model,

00:02:50.816 --> 00:02:52.490
but now we are shifting,

00:02:52.491 --> 00:02:53.930
very similar to social media,

00:02:53.931 --> 00:02:56.505
the change is happening in
the energy vertical as well.

00:02:56.506 --> 00:02:57.890
So, there is distributed

00:02:57.891 --> 00:03:00.460
solar and batteries which
is coming into picture,

00:03:00.461 --> 00:03:01.880
there are electric vehicles which

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:04.070
are basically getting
commercialized right now.

00:03:04.071 --> 00:03:06.020
So, it's completely changing

00:03:06.021 --> 00:03:08.750
the way the energy was
generated and consumed.

00:03:08.751 --> 00:03:12.200
So now, the commercial
or residential consumers

00:03:12.201 --> 00:03:13.915
are able to generate
their own energy,

00:03:13.916 --> 00:03:16.095
part of that they
might be consuming,

00:03:16.096 --> 00:03:17.450
part of that they can basically

00:03:17.451 --> 00:03:19.375
inject back into
the grid as well.

00:03:19.376 --> 00:03:21.760
So, it's not
a one way flow but now

00:03:21.761 --> 00:03:24.650
there is a two-way flow of
energy which is happening.

00:03:24.651 --> 00:03:28.205
Obviously, the
traditional power plants

00:03:28.206 --> 00:03:30.325
and traditional transmission
distribution network

00:03:30.326 --> 00:03:32.620
is not designed to handle

00:03:32.621 --> 00:03:34.164
this two-way type
of communication

00:03:34.165 --> 00:03:35.920
or two-way type of energy flow,

00:03:35.921 --> 00:03:37.960
and hence there are a lot of

00:03:37.961 --> 00:03:41.635
interesting socioeconomical
and technical challenges

00:03:41.636 --> 00:03:43.520
which are getting
created because of this.

00:03:43.521 --> 00:03:45.250
So, we will go through some of

00:03:45.251 --> 00:03:47.560
the issues in energy vertical and

00:03:47.561 --> 00:03:53.210
how the computer systems
like IoT and AI,

00:03:53.211 --> 00:03:55.575
can help solve some
of those issues.

00:03:55.576 --> 00:03:58.780
So, the first issue
is of power shortage.

00:03:58.781 --> 00:04:00.570
So, on the left-hand side,

00:04:00.571 --> 00:04:04.315
this is the power consumption,

00:04:04.316 --> 00:04:07.265
basically the power demand
from New York over the year.

00:04:07.266 --> 00:04:09.770
So, basically, this axis shows,

00:04:09.771 --> 00:04:10.960
over the year, how there are

00:04:10.961 --> 00:04:13.455
variations in the power demand.

00:04:13.456 --> 00:04:17.625
This particular axis shows
during a particular day,

00:04:17.626 --> 00:04:18.965
how the demand is varying.

00:04:18.966 --> 00:04:20.360
So, during a day, again,

00:04:20.361 --> 00:04:22.660
there are one or
two peaks depending upon

00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:24.535
the power consumption needs

00:04:24.536 --> 00:04:26.020
and depending upon the season,

00:04:26.021 --> 00:04:27.985
whether it's the summer
versus winter,

00:04:27.986 --> 00:04:30.100
there's a variability
in the demand.

00:04:30.101 --> 00:04:32.740
So now, if we look
at these plots,

00:04:32.741 --> 00:04:36.215
these are again from
the New York City.

00:04:36.216 --> 00:04:38.740
So, there was a highest
peak load demand here,

00:04:38.741 --> 00:04:42.250
which was 2006 versus the 1980's.

00:04:42.251 --> 00:04:44.720
If you see that, there
is no long tails

00:04:44.721 --> 00:04:46.570
in this kind of distribution,

00:04:46.571 --> 00:04:48.020
so these are basically

00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:50.545
hourly loads in terms
of megawatt hours,

00:04:50.546 --> 00:04:54.660
and as we are moving to
2006 and again to 2018,

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:57.585
we see very long tails of
demand which are there.

00:04:57.586 --> 00:04:59.575
So, the demand is increasing,

00:04:59.576 --> 00:05:02.580
there are short periods
of high demand,

00:05:02.581 --> 00:05:05.650
and definitely to cater
to these kind of demands,

00:05:05.651 --> 00:05:07.620
there is no point in adding

00:05:07.621 --> 00:05:09.180
the infrastructure just for

00:05:09.181 --> 00:05:11.400
catering to that
peak demand load,

00:05:11.401 --> 00:05:13.560
so definitely there is
some other methods or

00:05:13.561 --> 00:05:15.480
alternative methods are
required to curtail

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:17.730
toward address to
these kinds of demands,

00:05:17.731 --> 00:05:20.250
which are coming on
all the networks.

00:05:20.251 --> 00:05:23.075
The second problem is
of energy scarcity.

00:05:23.076 --> 00:05:24.600
So, in general,
there is a problem

00:05:24.601 --> 00:05:27.190
of access to clean
and cheap energy,

00:05:27.191 --> 00:05:28.900
so this is something which we

00:05:28.901 --> 00:05:31.960
have very much
experienced in India

00:05:31.961 --> 00:05:34.840
where there are regular power
cuts or there are places

00:05:34.841 --> 00:05:38.480
where there is no access
to energy as of now.

00:05:38.481 --> 00:05:41.620
So, this figure is from 2018,

00:05:41.621 --> 00:05:43.895
which shows that
across the world,

00:05:43.896 --> 00:05:47.705
how much population is without
electricity right now.

00:05:47.706 --> 00:05:50.795
As we speak right
now there is still

00:05:50.796 --> 00:05:53.140
approximately 300
million people in

00:05:53.141 --> 00:05:55.690
India who do not have
access to energy,

00:05:55.691 --> 00:05:57.430
and that's because
of various reasons

00:05:57.431 --> 00:05:59.020
of the cost which is required for

00:05:59.021 --> 00:06:00.580
the transmission
and distribution of

00:06:00.581 --> 00:06:03.205
the energy which is generated
at the central level,

00:06:03.206 --> 00:06:04.790
as well as the other problem

00:06:04.791 --> 00:06:07.430
of the availability
of energy itself.

00:06:07.431 --> 00:06:09.575
So current energy generation

00:06:09.576 --> 00:06:10.790
which is happening
through thermal

00:06:10.791 --> 00:06:12.140
and coal power plants in India,

00:06:12.141 --> 00:06:13.700
which is not sufficient to

00:06:13.701 --> 00:06:17.900
increasing demand of
energy at various places.

00:06:17.901 --> 00:06:21.120
The third issue that
we'll talk about is,

00:06:21.121 --> 00:06:22.850
mainly the good part which is

00:06:22.851 --> 00:06:24.485
happening is there is a lot of

00:06:24.486 --> 00:06:26.390
renewable and alternate
energy sources

00:06:26.391 --> 00:06:28.330
which are coming in
picture right now,

00:06:28.331 --> 00:06:30.395
in the form of solar,

00:06:30.396 --> 00:06:33.390
wind, as well as battery
storage et cetera.

00:06:33.391 --> 00:06:35.775
Good parts out there
are quite in local,

00:06:35.776 --> 00:06:37.070
so there are no transmission and

00:06:37.071 --> 00:06:38.685
distribution losses
involved here.

00:06:38.686 --> 00:06:40.755
They're are sustainable
and scalable.

00:06:40.756 --> 00:06:45.935
So you can scale
these plans as required.

00:06:45.936 --> 00:06:48.435
Most of these solar and,

00:06:48.436 --> 00:06:51.460
solar especially, is
reaching grid parity,

00:06:51.461 --> 00:06:53.740
which means that the
cost of generation

00:06:53.741 --> 00:06:56.350
of energy from solar which is

00:06:56.351 --> 00:06:59.740
becoming almost same
as cost of accessing

00:06:59.741 --> 00:07:01.075
the energy from the thermal

00:07:01.076 --> 00:07:03.555
or the existing
conventional plants.

00:07:03.556 --> 00:07:05.750
But there are some disadvantages

00:07:05.751 --> 00:07:08.940
in using these
renewable energy resources.

00:07:08.941 --> 00:07:11.170
The first problem is
generation variability.

00:07:11.171 --> 00:07:14.170
So, this plot shows
the generation

00:07:14.171 --> 00:07:17.885
across the year from one,
single wind turbine.

00:07:17.886 --> 00:07:20.190
So, as we see that there
is a huge variability

00:07:20.191 --> 00:07:22.920
across the year as well
as during one given day,

00:07:22.921 --> 00:07:24.300
there is a huge variability in

00:07:24.301 --> 00:07:26.400
the generation which is

00:07:26.401 --> 00:07:28.150
happening from
single wind turbine.

00:07:28.151 --> 00:07:30.060
So, when we are
taking into account

00:07:30.061 --> 00:07:33.450
the large adoption of
renewable energy resources,

00:07:33.451 --> 00:07:35.640
if we are not able to
predict how much amount of

00:07:35.641 --> 00:07:38.220
energy that will be
generated in next few hours,

00:07:38.221 --> 00:07:40.410
then we cannot do
the unit commitment and

00:07:40.411 --> 00:07:42.900
we cannot rely especially
on these kinds of sources.

00:07:42.901 --> 00:07:46.470
So, it's very important
that how reliably we can

00:07:46.471 --> 00:07:48.330
predict how much amount
of energy that would be

00:07:48.331 --> 00:07:50.565
generated from these
renewable energy resources,

00:07:50.566 --> 00:07:52.545
and it includes the

00:07:52.546 --> 00:07:55.200
accurate weather prediction
and on top of that,

00:07:55.201 --> 00:07:58.650
accurate prediction of
the energy from these sources.

00:07:58.651 --> 00:08:00.370
The second problem is that

00:08:00.371 --> 00:08:02.380
if you take into
account, let's say,

00:08:02.381 --> 00:08:04.380
solar, then the solar

00:08:04.381 --> 00:08:06.355
is available only during
few hours of day.

00:08:06.356 --> 00:08:07.950
So, it's available
only from, let's say,

00:08:07.951 --> 00:08:09.715
6.00 AM to 6.00 PM of the day,

00:08:09.716 --> 00:08:11.910
and again there is a
variability in amount

00:08:11.911 --> 00:08:15.090
of generation which is
happening from a solar plant.

00:08:15.091 --> 00:08:16.605
So, we cannot rely on

00:08:16.606 --> 00:08:19.710
this renewable energy
resources completely,

00:08:19.711 --> 00:08:22.200
we need to have additional
systems in place to

00:08:22.201 --> 00:08:25.435
backup whenever these
resources are not available,

00:08:25.436 --> 00:08:27.090
and that's where basically

00:08:27.091 --> 00:08:28.845
we are looking into,
storage technologies,

00:08:28.846 --> 00:08:30.500
and how storage and other

00:08:30.501 --> 00:08:32.720
technologies can be
integrated with renewables,

00:08:32.721 --> 00:08:35.960
so that we can reliably
provide power supply

00:08:35.961 --> 00:08:40.210
through these renewable
energy resources.

00:08:40.211 --> 00:08:43.130
So, that's where now we're

00:08:43.131 --> 00:08:45.160
going to talk about
the Participatory Grid.

00:08:45.161 --> 00:08:47.855
So now, the consumer is
not just the endpoint,

00:08:47.856 --> 00:08:50.250
consumer is not
the dumb or static consumer,

00:08:50.251 --> 00:08:53.060
but consumer is participating
in the entire process

00:08:53.061 --> 00:08:57.235
of energy generation and
consumption in various manners.

00:08:57.236 --> 00:09:02.260
So, the four main points that
I'm going to talk about is

00:09:02.261 --> 00:09:03.970
first is how we can address

00:09:03.971 --> 00:09:07.150
the power shortage problem
by reducing the peak demand?

00:09:07.151 --> 00:09:10.610
The second is how we can
address the energy scarcity?

00:09:10.611 --> 00:09:12.660
On one side there
is energy scarcity,

00:09:12.661 --> 00:09:15.150
which means that amount of
energy which is available,

00:09:15.151 --> 00:09:17.100
that is not sufficient
to cater to most

00:09:17.101 --> 00:09:19.300
of the energy resources,

00:09:19.301 --> 00:09:21.030
but on the other hand
we see lot of

00:09:21.031 --> 00:09:23.490
energy wastage which
happens in the residential,

00:09:23.491 --> 00:09:25.800
commercial and industrial places.

00:09:25.801 --> 00:09:27.300
So, can we identify

00:09:27.301 --> 00:09:28.590
the energy wastage and

00:09:28.591 --> 00:09:30.895
curtail those energy
wastage points?

00:09:30.896 --> 00:09:33.145
The third one is how we can

00:09:33.146 --> 00:09:35.485
basically maximize
the distributed generation,

00:09:35.486 --> 00:09:37.190
or basically adoption of

00:09:37.191 --> 00:09:39.380
distributed energy resources with

00:09:39.381 --> 00:09:40.880
the additional resources like

00:09:40.881 --> 00:09:42.470
solar and other technologies,

00:09:42.471 --> 00:09:44.090
and the final one is looking at

00:09:44.091 --> 00:09:45.925
optimal utilization of resources,

00:09:45.926 --> 00:09:48.740
which means that
basically including

00:09:48.741 --> 00:09:50.660
all the points of
preventive maintenance

00:09:50.661 --> 00:09:53.030
and predictive analytics.

00:09:53.031 --> 00:09:57.260
So, just going back
very quickly to

00:09:57.261 --> 00:09:59.340
IoT technologies and why

00:09:59.341 --> 00:10:01.130
IoT analytics is very suitable

00:10:01.131 --> 00:10:03.155
to solve some of these problems.

00:10:03.156 --> 00:10:05.900
So, we are looking at
basically the things which are

00:10:05.901 --> 00:10:08.450
equipment setup at most of

00:10:08.451 --> 00:10:11.405
these renewable or the
conventional energy resources.

00:10:11.406 --> 00:10:13.185
Most of these are capable of

00:10:13.186 --> 00:10:15.545
communicating data with
standard protocols.

00:10:15.546 --> 00:10:17.825
So, we do not have to,
most of the cases,

00:10:17.826 --> 00:10:20.995
we do not have to add
any new sensors as such,

00:10:20.996 --> 00:10:23.010
the existing batteries inverters,

00:10:23.011 --> 00:10:24.370
wind turbines or even

00:10:24.371 --> 00:10:25.900
the smart meters which

00:10:25.901 --> 00:10:27.440
are installed at
the home premises,

00:10:27.441 --> 00:10:28.740
they are capable of sending

00:10:28.741 --> 00:10:31.760
the data and the context
information as well.

00:10:31.761 --> 00:10:33.830
There are some existing processes

00:10:33.831 --> 00:10:35.180
in place which need to be

00:10:35.181 --> 00:10:39.620
adopted to basically look
into the new technologies.

00:10:39.621 --> 00:10:43.420
So, I like to basically look at

00:10:43.421 --> 00:10:47.835
the IoT stack similar
to brain architecture.

00:10:47.836 --> 00:10:50.790
So, as we look into the brain,

00:10:50.791 --> 00:10:52.110
there are different
systems which are

00:10:52.111 --> 00:10:53.820
responsible for different types

00:10:53.821 --> 00:10:57.130
of actions which are
performed in our body.

00:10:57.131 --> 00:11:00.775
So, if we look at
the Reptilian Brain,

00:11:00.776 --> 00:11:02.550
it's basically governing

00:11:02.551 --> 00:11:04.770
all the important or
survival instincts

00:11:04.771 --> 00:11:07.225
of human body including
breathing, heart rate,

00:11:07.226 --> 00:11:09.200
and balance, which are like

00:11:09.201 --> 00:11:13.090
low-latency response events which

00:11:13.091 --> 00:11:14.660
are required by the body.

00:11:14.661 --> 00:11:16.710
So, those events
are basically being

00:11:16.711 --> 00:11:20.575
handled very close to
the sensor systems.

00:11:20.576 --> 00:11:23.410
The second type of brain
is Limbic System which

00:11:23.411 --> 00:11:26.670
is looking at the feeling
and memory formation.

00:11:26.671 --> 00:11:30.170
So, looking at more data
and generally forming

00:11:30.171 --> 00:11:32.660
the patterns or memories through

00:11:32.661 --> 00:11:35.300
that and the last one
is basically,

00:11:35.301 --> 00:11:36.450
Neocortex which is doing

00:11:36.451 --> 00:11:40.020
the reasoning forward planning
language and other things.

00:11:40.021 --> 00:11:42.555
So, this is basically looking
at more holistically,

00:11:42.556 --> 00:11:45.220
the data from multiple sensors

00:11:45.221 --> 00:11:47.555
and making their
decisions on top of that.

00:11:47.556 --> 00:11:49.255
So, can we look at

00:11:49.256 --> 00:11:52.470
the IoT hierarchy of
systems in similar view.

00:11:52.471 --> 00:11:56.620
So, we have sensors and actuators
at the level which are,

00:11:56.621 --> 00:11:59.100
many times provided
by the equipment.

00:11:59.101 --> 00:12:01.120
Then, they have
the data loggers or

00:12:01.121 --> 00:12:03.090
edge devices which are sensing

00:12:03.091 --> 00:12:04.270
and collecting that data

00:12:04.271 --> 00:12:06.925
and capable of taking
certain actions.

00:12:06.926 --> 00:12:08.950
So, the actions which
are required as

00:12:08.951 --> 00:12:11.450
a low-latency response
actions or decisions which

00:12:11.451 --> 00:12:16.335
are crucial to be taken in
the very near real time,

00:12:16.336 --> 00:12:19.070
it's good to have
those decisions in

00:12:19.071 --> 00:12:21.880
the system which are as
close to edge as possible.

00:12:21.881 --> 00:12:25.110
Then, there are certain decisions

00:12:25.111 --> 00:12:28.000
which require information
from multiple resources.

00:12:28.001 --> 00:12:30.730
So, let's say, if we are
looking at the load across

00:12:30.731 --> 00:12:31.850
the community and some

00:12:31.851 --> 00:12:33.900
decisions that need to
be taken on top of that.

00:12:33.901 --> 00:12:35.960
Obviously, we would
not be able to take

00:12:35.961 --> 00:12:38.370
those decisions at
edge devices but it

00:12:38.371 --> 00:12:40.370
might be in the Fog
or the Local Data

00:12:40.371 --> 00:12:42.740
Center or the small
computing which is at,

00:12:42.741 --> 00:12:44.870
let say, community level where we

00:12:44.871 --> 00:12:47.225
can access that and
obviously, in the Cloud.

00:12:47.226 --> 00:12:50.560
So, there was some initial
work we did while I was at

00:12:50.561 --> 00:12:54.370
IBM on looking at
hierarchical decision system.

00:12:54.371 --> 00:12:57.715
So, can we do the analytics
and the hierarchical manner,

00:12:57.716 --> 00:12:59.580
where one node is doing

00:12:59.581 --> 00:13:01.860
the computation which
is possible and

00:13:01.861 --> 00:13:03.880
essential by
that particular node and

00:13:03.881 --> 00:13:06.265
passing on information to
the next level of nodes?

00:13:06.266 --> 00:13:08.060
So, depending upon the problem,

00:13:08.061 --> 00:13:11.400
can we decide where
the analytics should reside for

00:13:11.401 --> 00:13:12.970
that particular problem and can

00:13:12.971 --> 00:13:16.180
there be a connection
between those nodes?

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:20.420
So, we have not
completed this work

00:13:20.421 --> 00:13:21.960
after I left IBM research but

00:13:21.961 --> 00:13:23.485
we are interested
in taking it up.

00:13:23.486 --> 00:13:24.900
So, if there are any interest

00:13:24.901 --> 00:13:27.680
in the similar kind of systems,

00:13:27.681 --> 00:13:30.090
we'll be happy to
collaborate on this.

00:13:30.091 --> 00:13:32.895
So obviously,
the Neocortex is doing

00:13:32.896 --> 00:13:35.995
various types of analysis on
imaging time-series data.

00:13:35.996 --> 00:13:38.770
So, getting back to
the Participatory Grid,

00:13:38.771 --> 00:13:41.530
I'll start with the load
shifting problem which is

00:13:41.531 --> 00:13:45.210
basically looking at
Peak Power Demand.

00:13:45.211 --> 00:13:49.170
So, why reducing the
peak is important.

00:13:49.171 --> 00:13:50.670
So as we see,

00:13:50.671 --> 00:13:52.940
the X-axis is the time of day and

00:13:52.941 --> 00:13:55.920
Y-axis is the power
production or their demand.

00:13:55.921 --> 00:14:00.305
So, there are multiple peaks
during the day and

00:14:00.306 --> 00:14:03.010
the power is submitted
as in the power is

00:14:03.011 --> 00:14:06.250
generated by various different
types of load systems.

00:14:06.251 --> 00:14:09.470
One is basically Baseload
which could be a hydrothermal.

00:14:09.471 --> 00:14:10.800
So, which is a constant load

00:14:10.801 --> 00:14:12.510
which we are getting
through out the day.

00:14:12.511 --> 00:14:15.240
On top of that there is
an intermediate load,

00:14:15.241 --> 00:14:16.490
which is again provided by

00:14:16.491 --> 00:14:18.520
some conventional
energy resources

00:14:18.521 --> 00:14:19.990
and then, there are Peak Loads,

00:14:19.991 --> 00:14:22.205
which are typically provided by

00:14:22.206 --> 00:14:24.660
diesel generators which
have low response times,

00:14:24.661 --> 00:14:27.825
quickly can be started
and run through.

00:14:27.826 --> 00:14:30.570
But if we look on
the right side plot,

00:14:30.571 --> 00:14:34.130
it shows the amount of basically,

00:14:34.131 --> 00:14:37.570
the price versus
the power generation

00:14:37.571 --> 00:14:41.205
and the price does not
increase linearly.

00:14:41.206 --> 00:14:42.910
As we go to the peak demand,

00:14:42.911 --> 00:14:44.700
the price increases
exponentially.

00:14:44.701 --> 00:14:47.100
So, if we can reduce
this particular demand

00:14:47.101 --> 00:14:50.190
from a very small
tiny amount at least,

00:14:50.191 --> 00:14:53.080
it significantly helps
in reducing the price in

00:14:53.081 --> 00:14:54.240
this particular band when it

00:14:54.241 --> 00:14:56.315
goes exponentially increasing.

00:14:56.316 --> 00:14:59.300
So, there are historical
approaches which have been

00:14:59.301 --> 00:15:02.035
taken into account previously
to shift these loads.

00:15:02.036 --> 00:15:03.545
Some of these
approaches have been

00:15:03.546 --> 00:15:05.140
Daylight Saving Time or

00:15:05.141 --> 00:15:09.170
the Tokyo Brownouts and
CA Rolling Blackouts.

00:15:09.171 --> 00:15:11.140
Also in India, we see that there

00:15:11.141 --> 00:15:13.120
are these kind of
load shedding which

00:15:13.121 --> 00:15:15.140
happens like a few hours a day

00:15:15.141 --> 00:15:17.445
at different localities
to cater to the demand.

00:15:17.446 --> 00:15:19.890
So, some part of the community
would be completely

00:15:19.891 --> 00:15:22.545
shut down during
a particular point of time.

00:15:22.546 --> 00:15:25.310
So, this is more like
a top-down approach where

00:15:25.311 --> 00:15:26.760
the utility is deciding that I'm

00:15:26.761 --> 00:15:28.605
not able to get it
to the peak demand.

00:15:28.606 --> 00:15:30.470
So, I would shut down part of

00:15:30.471 --> 00:15:33.140
the community and that would be

00:15:33.141 --> 00:15:35.180
basically a ruling
load shaving that we

00:15:35.181 --> 00:15:38.960
see often during
the summer times.

00:15:38.961 --> 00:15:41.310
There are lot of other approaches

00:15:41.311 --> 00:15:43.520
which are being tried out in

00:15:43.521 --> 00:15:46.260
countries including US and Europe

00:15:46.261 --> 00:15:48.960
with variable degree of success,

00:15:48.961 --> 00:15:50.790
which are called Demand Response.

00:15:50.791 --> 00:15:54.250
So basically, if you look
at the left-hand side,

00:15:54.251 --> 00:15:58.610
it shows the typical
residential demand and what

00:15:58.611 --> 00:16:00.080
part of the residential demand

00:16:00.081 --> 00:16:03.300
is the deferrable or
time shiftable loads.

00:16:03.301 --> 00:16:05.910
So, if we're looking at
electrical vehicle charging,

00:16:05.911 --> 00:16:07.220
that could possibly be

00:16:07.221 --> 00:16:09.570
a time shiftable load
or if we're looking

00:16:09.571 --> 00:16:13.460
at water heater or
washing machines and dryers.

00:16:13.461 --> 00:16:15.780
Those are time
shiftable loads because

00:16:15.781 --> 00:16:18.030
it's not like you
need it immediately,

00:16:18.031 --> 00:16:21.945
but it can be done over a period
of three hours of time.

00:16:21.946 --> 00:16:24.550
So, there is a large proportion

00:16:24.551 --> 00:16:28.570
of loads in the residential,

00:16:28.571 --> 00:16:30.790
commercial and
industrial loads which

00:16:30.791 --> 00:16:33.300
are kind of time shiftable
and can be taken

00:16:33.301 --> 00:16:35.050
advantage of those loads to

00:16:35.051 --> 00:16:37.060
shift from pick-time to
off pick-time so that

00:16:37.061 --> 00:16:38.945
we can reduce the demand

00:16:38.946 --> 00:16:43.500
and basically do the peak
shaving during those times.

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:49.685
So, when we started
looking into basically,

00:16:49.686 --> 00:16:52.150
the problem of peak demand
and demand response for

00:16:52.151 --> 00:16:54.855
India or the
developing countries.

00:16:54.856 --> 00:16:58.020
The first challenges in
the way it's being tried

00:16:58.021 --> 00:17:01.090
out in US and Europe is,

00:17:01.091 --> 00:17:03.305
utility collects the data

00:17:03.306 --> 00:17:04.750
from the smart meters or

00:17:04.751 --> 00:17:06.410
individual appliances, let's say,

00:17:06.411 --> 00:17:09.165
washing machine is
in-demand response,

00:17:09.166 --> 00:17:11.520
then there is IoT device which is

00:17:11.521 --> 00:17:14.505
sitting on the washing machine.

00:17:14.506 --> 00:17:17.150
It's sending the data to
the Cloud and utility

00:17:17.151 --> 00:17:19.820
sends the demand response
signal either for

00:17:19.821 --> 00:17:21.725
the manual intervention or

00:17:21.726 --> 00:17:26.140
automatic dispatching the
load to the some other times.

00:17:26.141 --> 00:17:27.820
So, it requires basically

00:17:27.821 --> 00:17:30.680
complete infrastructure
for communication,

00:17:30.681 --> 00:17:33.020
centralize optimization
techniques where

00:17:33.021 --> 00:17:34.320
you can send the signals to

00:17:34.321 --> 00:17:36.930
the consumers and the consumers

00:17:36.931 --> 00:17:38.635
would participate
in demand response.

00:17:38.636 --> 00:17:42.930
There are few success stories
in certain utilities in

00:17:42.931 --> 00:17:45.050
US who have successfully done

00:17:45.051 --> 00:17:47.845
the demand response process
for shifting the load,

00:17:47.846 --> 00:17:51.205
but we started looking at
the same problem for let's say,

00:17:51.206 --> 00:17:55.010
India and Africa,
we do not even have

00:17:55.011 --> 00:17:56.620
the sufficient infrastructure

00:17:56.621 --> 00:17:58.825
for the network communication.

00:17:58.826 --> 00:18:01.550
So, we cannot rely on
the network communications to

00:18:01.551 --> 00:18:04.870
send the signal from the utility
to individual equipment.

00:18:04.871 --> 00:18:06.570
Also, there are lot of equipments

00:18:06.571 --> 00:18:08.220
which are already existing.

00:18:08.221 --> 00:18:10.405
So, we cannot change
those equipments,

00:18:10.406 --> 00:18:12.445
which means that there has
to be something which is

00:18:12.446 --> 00:18:15.280
sitting outside of the appliance,

00:18:15.281 --> 00:18:19.240
but still taking into account
the appliance preferences.

00:18:19.241 --> 00:18:21.860
The third thing that we
wanted to look into is,

00:18:21.861 --> 00:18:23.535
as to be as cheap as possible.

00:18:23.536 --> 00:18:24.810
So, it has to reside on

00:18:24.811 --> 00:18:28.420
a small device and
the third thing is,

00:18:28.421 --> 00:18:30.450
the same appliance should be

00:18:30.451 --> 00:18:32.830
applicable from
a range of appliances.

00:18:32.831 --> 00:18:33.840
So, it should not be that

00:18:33.841 --> 00:18:35.800
one type of appliance
is only running

00:18:35.801 --> 00:18:39.390
for water heaters versus
washing machines.

00:18:39.391 --> 00:18:41.630
So, it should be able to handle

00:18:41.631 --> 00:18:45.025
multiple different types of
time shiftable appliances.

00:18:45.026 --> 00:18:48.180
So, taking these multiple
things into account,

00:18:48.181 --> 00:18:50.070
we built something nPlug.

00:18:50.071 --> 00:18:52.110
Again, this is work which

00:18:52.111 --> 00:18:53.935
I did while I was
at IBM Research.

00:18:53.936 --> 00:18:56.010
So, what this nPlug does is,

00:18:56.011 --> 00:18:58.580
it's like a smart socket
or it's like

00:18:58.581 --> 00:19:01.755
over the power strip
that we generally use.

00:19:01.756 --> 00:19:04.180
So, it sits between the wall
socket and their appliance.

00:19:04.181 --> 00:19:07.080
The appliance goes
into the nPlug and

00:19:07.081 --> 00:19:10.849
this nPlug is built using
a 16-bit micro-controller,

00:19:10.850 --> 00:19:12.670
with very small,

00:19:12.671 --> 00:19:15.950
it could be of RAM and
four MB of flash memory.

00:19:15.951 --> 00:19:17.475
So, what it does is,

00:19:17.476 --> 00:19:19.910
it basically does local
sensing and decision-making.

00:19:19.911 --> 00:19:21.815
So, it senses the grid conditions

00:19:21.816 --> 00:19:24.140
especially of voltage
and frequency,

00:19:24.141 --> 00:19:25.500
to identify whether there

00:19:25.501 --> 00:19:27.845
is a high load on
the grid or not.

00:19:27.846 --> 00:19:32.305
It takes the preferences
from the consumer.

00:19:32.306 --> 00:19:34.980
So, let me take an
example of water heater.

00:19:34.981 --> 00:19:38.440
So, let's say most of
their time solar water heaters,

00:19:38.770 --> 00:19:42.180
the water can be kept
heated because there is

00:19:42.181 --> 00:19:44.160
insulation available for three to

00:19:44.161 --> 00:19:45.630
four hours in the morning.

00:19:45.631 --> 00:19:48.230
So, most of the times what
happens is we all get

00:19:48.231 --> 00:19:50.690
up at six o'clock and
start the water heaters.

00:19:50.691 --> 00:19:52.350
Water heater takes from

00:19:52.351 --> 00:19:54.365
two kilowatt to
four kilowatt of energy.

00:19:54.366 --> 00:19:56.520
Multiple such appliances come in

00:19:56.521 --> 00:19:59.935
the activation and we
see the morning peak.

00:19:59.936 --> 00:20:02.730
So, can we instead of starting,

00:20:02.731 --> 00:20:04.220
everyone starting and manually

00:20:04.221 --> 00:20:06.775
switching the
appliance at 6:00 AM,

00:20:06.776 --> 00:20:08.190
can we switch the appliance

00:20:08.191 --> 00:20:09.590
anytime between 3:00 to 6:00 AM,

00:20:09.591 --> 00:20:11.420
because water can be any way kept

00:20:11.421 --> 00:20:13.640
hot and ready for three hours.

00:20:13.641 --> 00:20:15.840
So, the user can set
the preference that,

00:20:15.841 --> 00:20:18.560
"I want the water to
be ready at 6:00 AM",

00:20:18.561 --> 00:20:21.180
and it needs to be run

00:20:21.181 --> 00:20:24.020
for 30 minutes let's say
for water to get heated.

00:20:24.021 --> 00:20:26.110
So, then we can actually shift

00:20:26.111 --> 00:20:28.150
this demand from 6:00 to 6:30 or

00:20:28.151 --> 00:20:30.070
whatever time the user
gets up and six to

00:20:30.071 --> 00:20:32.245
7:00 AM let's say it's
the peak demand time,

00:20:32.246 --> 00:20:35.325
we can actually do it anytime
from 3:00 to 6:00 AM,

00:20:35.326 --> 00:20:36.640
because that's the off-peak time,

00:20:36.641 --> 00:20:38.855
there is no much high
load on the grid.

00:20:38.856 --> 00:20:40.760
The user can set the preferences

00:20:40.761 --> 00:20:43.460
that 30 minutes is what I
want to run the appliance

00:20:43.461 --> 00:20:47.150
and 6:00 AM is

00:20:47.151 --> 00:20:49.500
the time by which I want
the water to be ready.

00:20:49.501 --> 00:20:51.500
Obviously, there are
"Override" buttons,

00:20:51.501 --> 00:20:54.865
so some days you don't
want to set that schedule,

00:20:54.866 --> 00:20:56.820
so you can always
override and say that I

00:20:56.821 --> 00:20:59.335
want to switch it on at
whatever time I want.

00:20:59.336 --> 00:21:01.875
So, without having
any external infrastructure

00:21:01.876 --> 00:21:03.430
and just having the sensing

00:21:03.431 --> 00:21:08.365
of voltage and frequency
inside the appliance itself,

00:21:08.366 --> 00:21:10.415
we wanted to see how we can

00:21:10.416 --> 00:21:13.200
identify the peak and
off-peak demand times.

00:21:13.201 --> 00:21:16.240
So, the first part is
an autonomous operation or

00:21:16.241 --> 00:21:18.210
decision making of identifying

00:21:18.211 --> 00:21:20.250
when there is
high demand on the grid.

00:21:20.251 --> 00:21:24.840
So, this plot shows the time
of day versus the voltage,

00:21:24.841 --> 00:21:28.185
and each color is
a different day of the week.

00:21:28.186 --> 00:21:31.650
So, this is the data

00:21:31.651 --> 00:21:34.360
which was collected from
my house in [inaudible].

00:21:34.361 --> 00:21:36.900
So, in the morning
you would see that

00:21:36.901 --> 00:21:39.710
the when night the voltage

00:21:39.711 --> 00:21:41.800
is high because these
off-peak times,

00:21:41.801 --> 00:21:43.845
the voltage is about 230 isn't

00:21:43.846 --> 00:21:46.990
nominal voltage we are expected
to have like 230 volts.

00:21:46.991 --> 00:21:49.300
But typically, at
night we will see

00:21:49.301 --> 00:21:52.065
it around 245 or 250 volts.

00:21:52.066 --> 00:21:55.335
Whereas in the evening
peak times around six to nine,

00:21:55.336 --> 00:21:58.310
you would see significant drop
which is like 220 volts.

00:21:58.311 --> 00:22:00.855
This was in Bangalore where

00:22:00.856 --> 00:22:04.500
this local transformer was
very close to my house.

00:22:04.501 --> 00:22:06.490
We did similar experiments at

00:22:06.491 --> 00:22:08.260
various different parts in India,

00:22:08.261 --> 00:22:10.610
and we see huge variability.

00:22:10.611 --> 00:22:13.875
So, if you go at some of the
rural places in [inaudible] ,

00:22:13.876 --> 00:22:15.380
you have seen the voltage
dropping up to

00:22:15.381 --> 00:22:17.945
180 volts or 175 volts.

00:22:17.946 --> 00:22:20.875
So, we cannot just fix a
particular range because a range

00:22:20.876 --> 00:22:22.410
varies across as you go

00:22:22.411 --> 00:22:24.535
from one location
to other location.

00:22:24.536 --> 00:22:27.135
So, we cannot just say
that anything above

00:22:27.136 --> 00:22:29.620
235 is the off-peak
time and anything

00:22:29.621 --> 00:22:32.220
below to 220 let say is

00:22:32.221 --> 00:22:35.080
the peak time because
depending upon the location,

00:22:35.081 --> 00:22:37.380
you have to adaptively
identify that what

00:22:37.381 --> 00:22:39.975
is the peak and
off-peak time range.

00:22:39.976 --> 00:22:44.855
So, we basically
collect the data at

00:22:44.856 --> 00:22:48.460
every 30 seconds or
one minute time interval and put

00:22:48.461 --> 00:22:50.060
the compression on top of it like

00:22:50.061 --> 00:22:52.220
this is aggregate approximation,

00:22:52.221 --> 00:22:54.950
for aggregating the signals

00:22:54.951 --> 00:22:57.130
at every five minutes
time interval.

00:22:57.131 --> 00:23:00.970
Then we look at basically
doing the clustering

00:23:00.971 --> 00:23:03.080
of these particular time series

00:23:03.081 --> 00:23:05.670
into three clusters of peak,

00:23:05.671 --> 00:23:08.045
off-peak, and the median time.

00:23:08.046 --> 00:23:09.605
Then we look at the patterns,

00:23:09.606 --> 00:23:12.500
so let's say
the algorithm looks at

00:23:12.501 --> 00:23:13.980
the last 10 days pattern

00:23:13.981 --> 00:23:17.060
to identify what are the
peak and off-peak times.

00:23:17.061 --> 00:23:20.430
So, based on that, it
decides that okay,

00:23:20.431 --> 00:23:23.055
it identifies first of
all the thresholds,

00:23:23.056 --> 00:23:24.390
which is the low threshold and

00:23:24.391 --> 00:23:25.865
upper threshold for voltage.

00:23:25.866 --> 00:23:27.720
Then identifies that based on

00:23:27.721 --> 00:23:30.270
last 10 days pattern that this is

00:23:30.271 --> 00:23:32.370
a time which is a peak time
and this is a time which

00:23:32.371 --> 00:23:35.620
off-peak when they need
to take the control.

00:23:35.621 --> 00:23:38.485
This wasn't previously
there were a lot of

00:23:38.486 --> 00:23:41.050
techniques which
were looking into

00:23:41.051 --> 00:23:43.050
the frequency data to identify

00:23:43.051 --> 00:23:45.690
these gaps between
the supply and demand,

00:23:45.691 --> 00:23:47.020
there was lot of work on this,

00:23:47.021 --> 00:23:48.240
but no one has looked into

00:23:48.241 --> 00:23:49.785
the voltage signals and buttons,

00:23:49.786 --> 00:23:52.065
so that was one of
the contributions.

00:23:52.066 --> 00:23:55.410
Doing these on
these small microcontrollers

00:23:55.411 --> 00:23:57.385
in streaming manner,
is what we did.

00:23:57.386 --> 00:23:59.450
The second thing is
looking at frequency.

00:23:59.451 --> 00:24:01.670
So, frequency would be ideally it

00:24:01.671 --> 00:24:04.935
should be 50 hertz and close to,

00:24:04.936 --> 00:24:07.345
if we look at
the data from yours,

00:24:07.346 --> 00:24:09.479
we would not see this
much of variability,

00:24:09.480 --> 00:24:11.900
it's pretty close to let
say 60 Hertz over there,

00:24:11.901 --> 00:24:13.910
but here we see

00:24:13.911 --> 00:24:18.200
a huge variability from
let's say 49.2-50.2.

00:24:18.201 --> 00:24:20.420
But still there are events when

00:24:20.421 --> 00:24:22.565
it goes beyond
the normal threshold.

00:24:22.566 --> 00:24:24.975
So, we identify what is
the normal behavior for

00:24:24.976 --> 00:24:28.080
frequency and which other times
where there is imbalance.

00:24:28.081 --> 00:24:30.750
So, if the demand is
high then generation,

00:24:30.751 --> 00:24:32.160
then the frequency will drop.

00:24:32.161 --> 00:24:33.610
So, we identify those events and

00:24:33.611 --> 00:24:36.390
again capture for
shifting the load.

00:24:36.391 --> 00:24:39.840
So, now we're able to identify

00:24:39.841 --> 00:24:42.250
the off-peak and peak times

00:24:42.251 --> 00:24:43.500
and we know that when we

00:24:43.501 --> 00:24:45.145
should not be running
the appliance.

00:24:45.146 --> 00:24:47.660
But let's say, now there
is no central controllers,

00:24:47.661 --> 00:24:49.090
as such every device is going

00:24:49.091 --> 00:24:50.830
to make the decision
independently.

00:24:50.831 --> 00:24:52.200
So, we should not create

00:24:52.201 --> 00:24:53.750
a problem of just
shifting the load

00:24:53.751 --> 00:24:55.220
from one time to another time

00:24:55.221 --> 00:24:57.220
by all of them
starting and shifting.

00:24:57.221 --> 00:24:58.590
So, let's say all of them sense,

00:24:58.591 --> 00:25:01.715
there are thousand influx and
all of them sense that it's

00:25:01.716 --> 00:25:04.070
a off-peak time and
all of them shift to

00:25:04.071 --> 00:25:06.810
another time and create
another peak demand.

00:25:06.811 --> 00:25:16.240
&gt;&gt; So, what's the disadvantage
of what the [inaudible].

00:25:16.241 --> 00:25:18.110
&gt;&gt; Basically, you are
ready to the peak demand.

00:25:18.111 --> 00:25:19.995
So, if you are running
the water heater

00:25:19.996 --> 00:25:22.940
and since utility is not
able to supply during

00:25:22.941 --> 00:25:25.060
that time because
there is a cap on

00:25:25.061 --> 00:25:27.005
how much energy utility can

00:25:27.006 --> 00:25:28.910
supply at any given
point of time.

00:25:28.911 --> 00:25:31.460
So, you would have
experience let's say

00:25:31.461 --> 00:25:34.585
morning and evening
power cuts, right?

00:25:34.586 --> 00:25:37.280
So, they typically do
the power cuts during peak time,

00:25:37.281 --> 00:25:38.690
because they are
not able to cater

00:25:38.691 --> 00:25:40.750
to the demand at that time.

00:25:40.751 --> 00:25:44.510
So, that's like
a top-down approach,

00:25:44.511 --> 00:25:46.555
they force everyone
to shut it off.

00:25:46.556 --> 00:25:48.400
But what this one is trying

00:25:48.401 --> 00:25:50.410
to do is like
a bottom-up approach

00:25:50.411 --> 00:25:52.970
that the device itself
says that I need

00:25:52.971 --> 00:25:55.750
not run only during
the peak time,

00:25:55.751 --> 00:25:58.585
so I can shift my demand.

00:25:58.586 --> 00:25:59.970
There are other loads rate,

00:25:59.971 --> 00:26:01.870
let say there are lights and

00:26:01.871 --> 00:26:03.500
air-conditioner and other things

00:26:03.501 --> 00:26:05.200
which are not time shiftable.

00:26:05.201 --> 00:26:08.875
So, we have to run those
appliances during peak time,

00:26:08.876 --> 00:26:10.375
so giving chance to

00:26:10.376 --> 00:26:13.310
the appliances which
definitely need to run during

00:26:13.311 --> 00:26:15.960
that time and shifting the loads

00:26:15.961 --> 00:26:19.075
which are kind of voluntarily,

00:26:19.076 --> 00:26:21.800
rather than utility forcing

00:26:21.801 --> 00:26:23.125
saying that
entire community would

00:26:23.126 --> 00:26:24.630
go down from 6:00
through 7:00 AM.

00:26:24.631 --> 00:26:27.600
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] ?

00:26:27.601 --> 00:26:30.200
&gt;&gt; So basically, we then
discussed with the utilities

00:26:30.201 --> 00:26:33.150
on how they can include as
part of the business model.

00:26:33.151 --> 00:26:36.444
So, for industrial consumers,

00:26:36.445 --> 00:26:40.205
there is time of
use pricing, right?

00:26:40.206 --> 00:26:41.709
So, even in Karnataka,

00:26:41.710 --> 00:26:44.610
there is a time of use
pricing where industries are

00:26:44.611 --> 00:26:47.800
charged higher rate if they
are running it on pick-time.

00:26:47.801 --> 00:26:49.850
So, we'll discuss
various business models if they

00:26:49.851 --> 00:26:52.150
can give a rebate if someone
is running the load.

00:26:52.151 --> 00:26:54.200
So, let say in
this particular devices

00:26:54.201 --> 00:26:57.270
and able to send the audit log
at the end of the month,

00:26:57.271 --> 00:27:01.210
which shows that it was not
running during the peak time.

00:27:01.211 --> 00:27:03.710
Then, there could be
discounts and rebates,

00:27:03.711 --> 00:27:06.260
as well as if there is
a time used pricing,

00:27:06.261 --> 00:27:07.510
there is a straight use case for

00:27:07.511 --> 00:27:10.060
the consumer to ship the
load during follow-up.

00:27:10.061 --> 00:27:30.000
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:27:30.001 --> 00:27:32.670
&gt;&gt; So, that's part of
the user preferences.

00:27:32.671 --> 00:27:34.210
So, there are certain loads which

00:27:34.211 --> 00:27:35.640
are like a long-running loads.

00:27:35.641 --> 00:27:37.580
So, if you take an
example of EV charging,

00:27:37.581 --> 00:27:39.195
that would be running
over four hours

00:27:39.196 --> 00:27:40.380
and you can basically

00:27:40.381 --> 00:27:43.870
chop that four hours into
let's say six hours with-.

00:27:43.871 --> 00:27:47.045
You can say that my window size
need to be 30 minutes,

00:27:47.046 --> 00:27:49.185
so you should not be
switching too often.

00:27:49.186 --> 00:27:51.310
So, if I give the chance

00:27:51.311 --> 00:27:53.440
for a plants were

00:27:53.441 --> 00:27:56.840
uninsured and for
at least minimum of 30 minutes,

00:27:56.841 --> 00:27:59.465
so that's part of the
user preferences.

00:27:59.466 --> 00:28:02.575
User can say, "Do I want
to run it at one go,

00:28:02.576 --> 00:28:03.880
which is a case for let say,

00:28:03.881 --> 00:28:06.310
washing machine that I
want to run it at one go?"

00:28:06.311 --> 00:28:10.200
versus if it's the
inverters that we charge

00:28:10.201 --> 00:28:11.625
at home or the

00:28:11.626 --> 00:28:14.165
electrical vehicles or
even the water heaters,

00:28:14.166 --> 00:28:15.740
where it can be chopped that

00:28:15.741 --> 00:28:19.270
the entire usage can be chopped
at multiple time windows.

00:28:19.271 --> 00:28:29.100
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:28:29.101 --> 00:28:31.380
&gt;&gt; No. So, the way
they had design it,

00:28:31.381 --> 00:28:33.585
user has to provide
the preferences.

00:28:33.586 --> 00:28:37.750
We wanted to keep minimal
inclusions in the device,

00:28:37.751 --> 00:28:39.570
taking into account
the small size of that.

00:28:39.571 --> 00:28:40.880
But that was a requirement

00:28:40.881 --> 00:28:44.940
from a few discussions
that we had that if

00:28:44.941 --> 00:28:48.580
the device is capable

00:28:48.581 --> 00:28:51.465
of providing the usage
pattern let's say.

00:28:51.466 --> 00:28:53.640
Let say, we can just collect

00:28:53.641 --> 00:28:55.590
the data of all consumption
of an appliance,

00:28:55.591 --> 00:28:57.690
can be categorize
what type of device it is?

00:28:57.691 --> 00:28:59.304
So, can we categorize,

00:28:59.305 --> 00:29:02.440
classify basically
the water heaters

00:29:02.441 --> 00:29:03.660
from washing machines and

00:29:03.661 --> 00:29:05.780
then automatically
put the preferences

00:29:05.781 --> 00:29:08.090
of what category of device it is?

00:29:08.091 --> 00:29:13.950
&gt;&gt; Is it different in
plant for each device?

00:29:13.951 --> 00:29:15.690
&gt;&gt; It's the same model,

00:29:15.691 --> 00:29:17.140
but if you are running it

00:29:17.141 --> 00:29:18.620
for multiple
different appliances,

00:29:18.621 --> 00:29:20.320
you would be putting
one for each.

00:29:20.321 --> 00:29:22.200
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:29:22.201 --> 00:29:24.225
&gt;&gt; In this model, it was not.

00:29:24.226 --> 00:29:26.480
But then there was
some conversation on,

00:29:26.481 --> 00:29:29.510
let's say there are 10 templates
within one industry.

00:29:29.511 --> 00:29:31.110
Can they talk to
each other and do

00:29:31.111 --> 00:29:34.990
some even better optimization
in terms of distribution?

00:29:34.991 --> 00:29:46.960
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:29:46.961 --> 00:29:48.880
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, they did

00:29:48.881 --> 00:29:51.555
these experiments on
the data collected from,

00:29:51.556 --> 00:29:53.625
let's say, US houses as well.

00:29:53.626 --> 00:29:56.835
As I said, the range was small.

00:29:56.836 --> 00:29:59.570
So, even in case of
frequency and voltage,

00:29:59.571 --> 00:30:00.880
the range is very tight,

00:30:00.881 --> 00:30:02.490
but still, you would
see the pattern.

00:30:02.491 --> 00:30:04.520
So, the pattern is available
over there tight range.

00:30:04.521 --> 00:30:15.390
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] ?

00:30:15.391 --> 00:30:18.060
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, you would see
a small variability still.

00:30:18.061 --> 00:30:20.600
So, the variability
might be in, let's say,

00:30:20.601 --> 00:30:23.460
then this two minus
two but still,

00:30:23.461 --> 00:30:27.700
the variability is
available over there.

00:30:27.701 --> 00:30:30.320
Basically, to control
this lot of utilities,

00:30:30.321 --> 00:30:32.370
put additional devices for doing

00:30:32.371 --> 00:30:34.760
multi-stablization and
frequency stabilization.

00:30:34.761 --> 00:30:37.570
So, a lot of rather in India,

00:30:37.571 --> 00:30:39.650
while the utilities, or

00:30:39.651 --> 00:30:42.095
I think in few countries
in Europe as well,

00:30:42.096 --> 00:30:46.970
the pricing in the
real-time energy market,

00:30:46.971 --> 00:30:48.620
the amount of price that you get

00:30:48.621 --> 00:30:50.710
paid for providing energy

00:30:50.711 --> 00:30:54.350
at a particular point
of time is called ABT,

00:30:54.351 --> 00:30:55.860
availability-based tariff,

00:30:55.861 --> 00:30:57.920
that's dependent on
the frequency at that time.

00:30:57.921 --> 00:30:59.340
So, if the frequency is low and

00:30:59.341 --> 00:31:00.975
if you're supplying
energy at that time,

00:31:00.976 --> 00:31:03.250
you get paid more versus

00:31:03.251 --> 00:31:05.610
when if you are supplying
in this particular region.

00:31:05.611 --> 00:31:16.450
&gt;&gt;  [inaudible].

00:31:16.451 --> 00:31:18.845
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, again,

00:31:18.846 --> 00:31:21.600
it's not widely
available as of now.

00:31:21.601 --> 00:31:24.715
It was initially done
as part of the pilots.

00:31:24.716 --> 00:31:28.140
Now, as they're
putting smart meters,

00:31:28.141 --> 00:31:30.320
the demand response
is generally done if

00:31:30.321 --> 00:31:32.270
there is smart meters available.

00:31:32.271 --> 00:31:34.460
But a smart meters giving
you timestamp data

00:31:34.461 --> 00:31:35.890
already and being pushed to

00:31:35.891 --> 00:31:37.485
the Cloud almost in real-time,

00:31:37.486 --> 00:31:40.130
so that's where the analysis
is happening in the Cloud.

00:31:40.131 --> 00:31:48.610
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] ?

00:31:48.611 --> 00:31:51.340
&gt;&gt; So, I'll go to
the other problem where

00:31:51.341 --> 00:31:54.355
we do look at the
power consumption of the plants.

00:31:54.356 --> 00:31:56.330
But in this case, it was mainly

00:31:56.331 --> 00:32:00.570
for identifying what is
the condition of the grid,

00:32:00.571 --> 00:32:02.515
whether the grid is
overloaded or not.

00:32:02.516 --> 00:32:04.560
So, if you look at the
power consumption part,

00:32:04.561 --> 00:32:05.830
you will just get
to know that how

00:32:05.831 --> 00:32:07.630
your appliances working
but you would not

00:32:07.631 --> 00:32:09.760
get to know that your transformer

00:32:09.761 --> 00:32:11.105
is overloaded right now,

00:32:11.106 --> 00:32:13.185
and you need to basically

00:32:13.186 --> 00:32:16.230
curtail your demand at
this particular point of time.

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:20.375
So, basically, once
the problem of

00:32:20.376 --> 00:32:22.745
identifying the good
condition was solved,

00:32:22.746 --> 00:32:25.580
then we wanted to look
at how we can basically

00:32:25.581 --> 00:32:29.745
distribute spread
that demand over time.

00:32:29.746 --> 00:32:30.895
So, we looked at

00:32:30.896 --> 00:32:33.995
various decentralized
scheduling techniques.

00:32:33.996 --> 00:32:37.140
We basically looked
at computer networks,

00:32:37.141 --> 00:32:38.850
CSMA kind of protocols,

00:32:38.851 --> 00:32:40.740
which tried to back off

00:32:40.741 --> 00:32:43.635
when there is a high demand
on the network.

00:32:43.636 --> 00:32:46.510
So, we call this algorithm as

00:32:46.511 --> 00:32:48.180
a Grid Sense Multiple
Access similar to

00:32:48.181 --> 00:32:50.200
Carrier Sense Multiple Access.

00:32:50.201 --> 00:32:52.525
There are few
differences in this one.

00:32:52.526 --> 00:32:53.985
So, if we look at this

00:32:53.986 --> 00:32:55.760
similar computer
network algorithms,

00:32:55.761 --> 00:32:59.665
there is no deadline for
completing the packet transport.

00:32:59.666 --> 00:33:04.085
So, you can basically attempt
and send the packets,

00:33:04.086 --> 00:33:06.380
whereas in this problem,
there is a deadline.

00:33:06.381 --> 00:33:07.710
So, if I'm saying that I want

00:33:07.711 --> 00:33:09.490
the water to be ready at 6:00 AM,

00:33:09.491 --> 00:33:11.260
then 6:00 AM is my deadline.

00:33:11.261 --> 00:33:13.000
I need to, no matter what,

00:33:13.001 --> 00:33:14.480
we want to give preference to

00:33:14.481 --> 00:33:17.130
the user's deadlines which
means that the water

00:33:17.131 --> 00:33:21.785
has to be ready by
the deadline which is given.

00:33:21.786 --> 00:33:24.820
So, we had to basically
do some modifications for

00:33:24.821 --> 00:33:28.930
CSMA algorithms to suit to
the deadline-based algorithms.

00:33:28.931 --> 00:33:32.545
So, the first thing that we
did was contention window,

00:33:32.546 --> 00:33:35.760
depending upon
the available time slots.

00:33:35.761 --> 00:33:37.600
So, let's say if I am at 3:00 AM

00:33:37.601 --> 00:33:39.655
and I have a 30-minute slot.

00:33:39.656 --> 00:33:41.480
So, I have three hours
which means

00:33:41.481 --> 00:33:43.580
six slots which are available
or five minutes slots,

00:33:43.581 --> 00:33:45.845
whatever number of slots
which are available,

00:33:45.846 --> 00:33:48.470
the current time slot during that

00:33:48.471 --> 00:33:50.260
particular 3:00-6:00 AM window

00:33:50.261 --> 00:33:51.995
and how much time I
need to run that.

00:33:51.996 --> 00:33:53.175
So, depending upon that,

00:33:53.176 --> 00:33:55.160
every device basically tries

00:33:55.161 --> 00:33:57.360
to identify
the contention window.

00:33:57.361 --> 00:33:58.950
As the time elapses,

00:33:58.951 --> 00:34:01.625
as you start going
towards their deadline,

00:34:01.626 --> 00:34:03.185
your contention window size

00:34:03.186 --> 00:34:05.070
starts becoming
smaller and smaller.

00:34:05.071 --> 00:34:07.210
So, what the device does is,

00:34:07.211 --> 00:34:10.525
let's say if I have seven as
the contention window size,

00:34:10.526 --> 00:34:13.580
device chooses randomly
to sense at any

00:34:13.581 --> 00:34:16.480
of these initial
seven-minute slots,

00:34:16.481 --> 00:34:18.390
and identify sense the grid and

00:34:18.391 --> 00:34:21.395
identifies whether it's
the loaded or not.

00:34:21.396 --> 00:34:22.675
So, by doing this, we are

00:34:22.676 --> 00:34:24.340
first of all
distributing the amount

00:34:24.341 --> 00:34:25.720
of endplates with
just sensing and

00:34:25.721 --> 00:34:27.605
making their decision
at the same time.

00:34:27.606 --> 00:34:32.615
The second thing is
the probabilistic connection.

00:34:32.616 --> 00:34:35.360
So, depending upon
the voltage levels,

00:34:35.361 --> 00:34:40.555
so let's say the voltage
is below peak load,

00:34:40.556 --> 00:34:42.200
then it says that you

00:34:42.201 --> 00:34:44.120
should not be joining
because it's a peak time.

00:34:44.121 --> 00:34:46.090
If it's above
the upper threshold,

00:34:46.091 --> 00:34:47.650
which means that
it's a peak time and

00:34:47.651 --> 00:34:50.040
device can definitely join
at any point of time.

00:34:50.041 --> 00:34:52.800
If it's in-between,
then basically,

00:34:52.801 --> 00:34:55.290
it computes
probabilistically that with

00:34:55.291 --> 00:34:58.305
how much probability that you
should be joining the grid,

00:34:58.306 --> 00:34:59.935
whether you should be starting.

00:34:59.936 --> 00:35:04.995
So, by doing these
probabilistic arrangements,

00:35:04.996 --> 00:35:06.840
not all endplates
would again join

00:35:06.841 --> 00:35:08.550
at the same time
but they distribute

00:35:08.551 --> 00:35:10.960
probabilistically and would not

00:35:10.961 --> 00:35:13.580
increase the load beyond
a critical point.

00:35:13.581 --> 00:35:25.140
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] ?

00:35:25.141 --> 00:35:27.445
&gt;&gt; So, we did some experiments
on let's say if

00:35:27.446 --> 00:35:32.730
there are two controllers,

00:35:32.731 --> 00:35:34.500
two endplate controllers
in one house.

00:35:34.501 --> 00:35:37.800
Obviously, if they can
communicate between each other,

00:35:37.801 --> 00:35:40.565
there would be better chance

00:35:40.566 --> 00:35:42.265
of optimization than this one.

00:35:42.266 --> 00:35:43.820
If you are able to
do it centrally,

00:35:43.821 --> 00:35:45.585
that would be a complete
optimal solution.

00:35:45.586 --> 00:35:51.090
So, as we go up in the hierarchy
or from the H242 Cloud,

00:35:51.091 --> 00:35:52.260
it might be giving you a bit.

00:35:52.261 --> 00:35:54.655
Because you have higher
information available with you,

00:35:54.656 --> 00:35:56.860
you would be able to do
better optimization.

00:35:56.861 --> 00:35:59.045
By taking into account
the constraints of,

00:35:59.046 --> 00:36:01.040
we didn't want to write any
communication infrastructure,

00:36:01.041 --> 00:36:04.825
not even the connection with
Wi-Fi or the other points.

00:36:04.826 --> 00:36:07.920
So, because of that, we
decided that we would make

00:36:07.921 --> 00:36:09.410
their decisions
locally and we would

00:36:09.411 --> 00:36:11.700
see how much the
difference it comes to.

00:36:11.701 --> 00:36:13.690
If there are not many sensor,

00:36:13.691 --> 00:36:15.520
if there are not
hundreds or thousands of

00:36:15.521 --> 00:36:17.820
sensors being controlled
at one location,

00:36:17.821 --> 00:36:21.270
this one still gives the same
performance as if it's two,

00:36:21.271 --> 00:36:23.685
10, or 100 still gives
the same performance.

00:36:23.686 --> 00:36:24.550
&gt;&gt; For households, this
is still okay to that?

00:36:24.551 --> 00:36:30.475
&gt;&gt; Well, even households

00:36:30.476 --> 00:36:34.485
and industries which have
less than 100 or 200 devices,

00:36:34.486 --> 00:36:36.460
this still works equally as if

00:36:36.461 --> 00:36:41.920
you had taken
the centralized decision.

00:36:41.921 --> 00:36:42.930
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] ?

00:36:43.650 --> 00:36:47.400
&gt;&gt; So, that's another as in I
can point you to the paper.

00:36:47.401 --> 00:36:49.440
Where looking at users load.

00:36:49.441 --> 00:36:51.780
We tried to identify
what are the preferences

00:36:51.781 --> 00:36:54.840
of running their devices and

00:36:54.841 --> 00:36:56.730
based on the context of how they

00:36:56.731 --> 00:36:58.760
would like to run
their devices over weekends

00:36:58.761 --> 00:37:03.710
versus let's say weekdays
and other things.

00:37:03.711 --> 00:37:05.265
So, these are the results of,

00:37:05.266 --> 00:37:07.010
we tried it in simulation and

00:37:07.011 --> 00:37:10.115
some controlled experiments
in real world.

00:37:10.116 --> 00:37:12.290
So, basically here it's

00:37:12.291 --> 00:37:14.840
creating two peaks
during the day.

00:37:14.841 --> 00:37:17.050
There are different
types of inputs

00:37:17.051 --> 00:37:19.380
like water pumps and
washing machines,

00:37:19.381 --> 00:37:21.425
water heaters and inverters,

00:37:21.426 --> 00:37:23.190
which have variable preferences

00:37:23.191 --> 00:37:24.800
in terms of their time window,

00:37:24.801 --> 00:37:26.720
how much they want to run?

00:37:26.721 --> 00:37:30.815
The starting times and
durations and other things.

00:37:30.816 --> 00:37:33.680
So, we are considering
let say close to I think

00:37:33.681 --> 00:37:37.715
400 or 4,000 of such appliances.

00:37:37.716 --> 00:37:39.770
Even when they are taking

00:37:39.771 --> 00:37:42.460
these independent decision
in a decentralized manner,

00:37:42.461 --> 00:37:46.020
because now they can shift
this particular peak

00:37:46.021 --> 00:37:48.280
spent three hours or

00:37:48.281 --> 00:37:51.405
four hours before
the actual time duration.

00:37:51.406 --> 00:37:53.300
It's able to actually

00:37:53.301 --> 00:37:57.440
significantly spread
the demand over time.

00:37:57.441 --> 00:37:58.970
&gt;&gt; is this simulation or
is it actual measurement?

00:37:58.971 --> 00:38:00.245
&gt;&gt; This was in simulation.

00:38:00.246 --> 00:38:02.720
So, we did various simulation and

00:38:02.721 --> 00:38:04.460
then we tried it in

00:38:04.461 --> 00:38:07.280
the control experiments
of 100 devices.

00:38:07.281 --> 00:38:11.020
So, 100 devices we ran on

00:38:11.021 --> 00:38:13.365
multiple different appliances and

00:38:13.366 --> 00:38:15.810
users said there are
preferences as their need.

00:38:15.811 --> 00:38:17.500
So, we didn't have control over

00:38:17.501 --> 00:38:20.260
how they said the preferences.

00:38:21.280 --> 00:38:24.220
So, now we're moving
from the load

00:38:24.221 --> 00:38:27.140
shifting to energy
efficiency problem.

00:38:27.141 --> 00:38:30.120
So, there is one thing is

00:38:30.121 --> 00:38:31.360
there is high demand
which utility

00:38:31.361 --> 00:38:32.695
is not able to get into,

00:38:32.696 --> 00:38:34.520
but on the other side,
we see that there is

00:38:34.521 --> 00:38:36.700
lot of wastage of
electricity which happens.

00:38:36.701 --> 00:38:40.010
The wastage could be because
of the aging appliances like

00:38:40.011 --> 00:38:44.600
Aging refrigerators or
the cooling systems.

00:38:44.601 --> 00:38:48.010
In US itself
30 to 45 percentage of

00:38:48.011 --> 00:38:49.510
each HVAC system had to

00:38:49.511 --> 00:38:52.205
operate at below
efficiency levels.

00:38:52.206 --> 00:38:55.010
So, this could be again
because of two reasons;

00:38:55.011 --> 00:38:57.150
one is that the malfunctioning
or aging of their

00:38:57.151 --> 00:38:59.510
appliance or inefficient
use of appliance.

00:38:59.511 --> 00:39:01.225
So, it could be that

00:39:01.226 --> 00:39:02.870
the air conditioner is running

00:39:02.871 --> 00:39:04.510
by individuals and
doors are open.

00:39:04.511 --> 00:39:07.930
Which means that it's not
running at the optimum levels.

00:39:07.931 --> 00:39:10.340
To address this problem,

00:39:10.341 --> 00:39:12.380
what we did was
something similar where

00:39:12.381 --> 00:39:14.325
it's called as a SocketWatch.

00:39:14.326 --> 00:39:15.620
Which again sits between

00:39:15.621 --> 00:39:17.665
the appliance and
the wall socket,

00:39:17.666 --> 00:39:20.510
and now it looks at instead
of looking at the grid,

00:39:20.511 --> 00:39:22.005
it looks at the power consumption

00:39:22.006 --> 00:39:23.415
of that particular appliance.

00:39:23.416 --> 00:39:25.970
So, we collect data of
active or reactive power and

00:39:25.971 --> 00:39:27.900
various other parameters
which can be sensed

00:39:27.901 --> 00:39:30.245
from outside of their plants.

00:39:30.246 --> 00:39:31.640
Then we try to model

00:39:31.641 --> 00:39:34.090
the benchmark model for
that particular plant.

00:39:34.091 --> 00:39:35.240
So, here is an example

00:39:35.241 --> 00:39:38.030
of washing machine which
goes through different modes

00:39:38.031 --> 00:39:39.925
depending upon
the settings that you

00:39:39.926 --> 00:39:42.655
choose on the the
washing machine.

00:39:42.656 --> 00:39:46.805
It has different signatures
of active or reactive power.

00:39:46.806 --> 00:39:50.435
So, we identified these
states of their plants,

00:39:50.436 --> 00:39:53.490
whatever appliances running
in during the benchmark time.

00:39:53.491 --> 00:39:55.820
So, this is a learning phase
when we collect

00:39:55.821 --> 00:39:58.380
the data and build
a benchmark model.

00:39:58.381 --> 00:40:00.770
So, here it's basically

00:40:00.771 --> 00:40:03.290
showing that there are
different states of

00:40:03.291 --> 00:40:06.100
power consumption of
their plants and how

00:40:06.101 --> 00:40:07.700
the states are transitioned from

00:40:07.701 --> 00:40:10.000
one state to another
as a Macro model.

00:40:10.001 --> 00:40:12.860
So, with the probabilities
of going from one state to

00:40:12.861 --> 00:40:16.430
another state depending
upon various factors.

00:40:16.431 --> 00:40:20.370
So, there are two phases,

00:40:20.371 --> 00:40:22.825
one is the learning phase
when we identify the modes,

00:40:22.826 --> 00:40:25.235
depending upon the active or
reactive power consumption.

00:40:25.236 --> 00:40:26.790
The states depending upon

00:40:26.791 --> 00:40:28.945
the time spent in
each of the mode.

00:40:28.946 --> 00:40:30.820
So, sometimes the same

00:40:30.821 --> 00:40:32.520
power consumption
but if it's running

00:40:32.521 --> 00:40:34.105
for different time
intervals means

00:40:34.106 --> 00:40:36.560
differently or it's
a different state.

00:40:36.561 --> 00:40:39.590
Then we look at the periodicity
between those modes.

00:40:39.591 --> 00:40:43.690
So, for example if we are
taking example of refrigerator,

00:40:43.691 --> 00:40:46.695
the defrost heating might
be running once every day.

00:40:46.696 --> 00:40:48.300
So, that's the
periodicity of that.

00:40:48.301 --> 00:40:53.360
Or, if we see the cycling
of compressor,

00:40:53.361 --> 00:40:56.090
it might be running at
specific time intervals,

00:40:56.091 --> 00:40:58.120
which is a periodicity and
then the state transition.

00:40:58.121 --> 00:40:59.420
So, how the device

00:40:59.421 --> 00:41:01.735
transitions from one state
to another state.

00:41:01.736 --> 00:41:04.505
In the model, once you
have the benchmark model,

00:41:04.506 --> 00:41:06.620
in the monitoring phase
we do two things;

00:41:06.621 --> 00:41:08.145
one is the standby
mode correction.

00:41:08.146 --> 00:41:09.770
So, if we identify that

00:41:09.771 --> 00:41:12.105
the appliances is
running on standby,

00:41:12.106 --> 00:41:14.500
then it's switched off and if

00:41:14.501 --> 00:41:16.080
we identify that there is

00:41:16.081 --> 00:41:17.740
a change from
the benchmark states,

00:41:17.741 --> 00:41:19.820
then we basically alert as

00:41:19.821 --> 00:41:23.690
a malfunction or inefficient
use of the appliance.

00:41:25.270 --> 00:41:30.660
So, every appliance while
it consumes the power,

00:41:30.661 --> 00:41:32.960
it consumes the power
in two forms.

00:41:32.961 --> 00:41:34.550
So, one is the,

00:41:34.551 --> 00:41:40.385
so basically the breakage
of your power consumption.

00:41:40.386 --> 00:41:42.490
Which is termed as it has

00:41:42.491 --> 00:41:45.270
a phase angle and it basically
gives you two parameters.

00:41:45.271 --> 00:41:46.980
That how much is
active or reactive power,

00:41:46.981 --> 00:41:48.510
depending upon if your load is

00:41:48.511 --> 00:41:50.230
inductive or was capacitive.

00:41:50.231 --> 00:41:53.400
The amount of active or
reactive power would change.

00:41:53.401 --> 00:41:56.740
So, the combination of
active or reactive power would

00:41:56.741 --> 00:41:58.290
define in some sense what kind of

00:41:58.291 --> 00:42:00.460
operation is happening
in your plants.

00:42:00.461 --> 00:42:12.560
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:42:12.561 --> 00:42:14.420
&gt;&gt; So, basically if you

00:42:14.421 --> 00:42:17.215
measure any appliance's
power consumption,

00:42:17.216 --> 00:42:19.960
you get these two factors of
active or reactive power.

00:42:19.961 --> 00:42:21.525
So, it's just sitting outside

00:42:21.526 --> 00:42:24.045
and measuring power consumption,
that's all it's doing.

00:42:24.046 --> 00:42:25.580
So, there is a lag between,

00:42:25.581 --> 00:42:27.110
as in it's power factor between

00:42:27.111 --> 00:42:30.140
the active or reactive power

00:42:30.141 --> 00:42:32.520
which is a phase angle of those.

00:42:33.400 --> 00:42:35.930
So, here are some of

00:42:35.931 --> 00:42:38.320
the examples of
identifying the issues.

00:42:38.321 --> 00:42:41.240
So, this particular one
of the refrigerators,

00:42:41.241 --> 00:42:43.190
as in this is the
benchmark model.

00:42:43.191 --> 00:42:45.980
When you see that
there is typically,

00:42:45.981 --> 00:42:48.125
there are states like
compressor cycling,

00:42:48.126 --> 00:42:49.605
compressor going on and off.

00:42:49.606 --> 00:42:54.825
Which if you look
into the clusters,

00:42:54.826 --> 00:42:57.330
it basically identifies
different clusters

00:42:57.331 --> 00:43:00.115
which are states in terms
of power consumption.

00:43:00.116 --> 00:43:01.539
There is a periodicity,

00:43:01.540 --> 00:43:02.960
so how much amount it stays in

00:43:02.961 --> 00:43:05.955
the compressor on mode
versus compressor off mode.

00:43:05.956 --> 00:43:08.390
This refrigerator after building

00:43:08.391 --> 00:43:11.180
benchmark model,
we identified it.

00:43:11.181 --> 00:43:14.320
We basically changing
the states and

00:43:14.321 --> 00:43:15.750
then they look back and found

00:43:15.751 --> 00:43:17.710
out that there was
a gasket leakage.

00:43:17.711 --> 00:43:19.060
So, what's happening is,

00:43:19.061 --> 00:43:20.740
since there is a gasket leakage,

00:43:20.741 --> 00:43:22.465
it's not cooling sufficiently.

00:43:22.466 --> 00:43:24.745
So, it's not able to
take its cooling,

00:43:24.746 --> 00:43:26.440
so it was not identified by

00:43:26.441 --> 00:43:28.370
the user because it seems to be

00:43:28.371 --> 00:43:30.110
working fine but it

00:43:30.111 --> 00:43:32.200
wasn't ever going into
the compressor off mode,

00:43:32.201 --> 00:43:33.700
because thermostat is not able

00:43:33.701 --> 00:43:35.345
to activate the
compressor off mode.

00:43:35.346 --> 00:43:37.980
So, if you see here,
it's always on and

00:43:37.981 --> 00:43:40.945
there are no cycles of on
and off kind of a thing.

00:43:40.946 --> 00:43:45.855
If we look into
the clustering or the states,

00:43:45.856 --> 00:43:47.760
here was the
compressor off state,

00:43:47.761 --> 00:43:49.190
which was coming periodically,

00:43:49.191 --> 00:43:50.685
which was completely missing

00:43:50.686 --> 00:43:53.540
from the actual
operational model.

00:43:54.490 --> 00:43:57.100
So, just moving next

00:43:57.101 --> 00:43:59.625
to the distributed
generation part.

00:43:59.626 --> 00:44:03.690
So, basically, these
are called Microgrids,

00:44:03.691 --> 00:44:06.460
so the examples which I
talked about earlier.

00:44:06.461 --> 00:44:08.400
Because they are
doing the generation

00:44:08.401 --> 00:44:10.060
and consumption at
the same location,

00:44:10.061 --> 00:44:11.900
so they are called Microgrids.

00:44:11.901 --> 00:44:14.770
There are various forms of
Microgrids like Offline,

00:44:14.771 --> 00:44:16.560
which are completely
working independently,

00:44:16.561 --> 00:44:18.695
or there are Backup or Online,

00:44:18.696 --> 00:44:20.740
which are typically
the solar storage

00:44:20.741 --> 00:44:22.380
in the house where you are

00:44:22.381 --> 00:44:24.100
getting grid power
as well as you have

00:44:24.101 --> 00:44:26.855
your own system of the grid.

00:44:26.856 --> 00:44:30.825
It has been historically,
we can say,

00:44:30.826 --> 00:44:32.120
the consumption and generation

00:44:32.121 --> 00:44:33.060
happening at the same place,

00:44:33.061 --> 00:44:34.810
so they're historically
different Microgrids,

00:44:34.811 --> 00:44:36.840
but obviously, those
are not efficient.

00:44:36.841 --> 00:44:39.955
So, the problem that
we discussed earlier,

00:44:39.956 --> 00:44:42.955
previously, the user were
just doing consumption.

00:44:42.956 --> 00:44:45.125
So, it was simple on
and off of a switch.

00:44:45.126 --> 00:44:48.270
Whereas now, when the consumer
is called the Prosumer,

00:44:48.271 --> 00:44:52.130
where there is a local
generation and consumption,

00:44:52.131 --> 00:44:53.720
the complexity of the problem is

00:44:53.721 --> 00:44:56.165
increasing on the consumer side.

00:44:56.166 --> 00:44:58.500
So, this is like a time of

00:44:58.501 --> 00:45:00.660
use pricing which is
provided by the utility.

00:45:00.661 --> 00:45:02.480
So, depending upon
whether it's a summer

00:45:02.481 --> 00:45:04.515
or whether it's a weekday
versus weekend,

00:45:04.516 --> 00:45:07.930
there are different prices
for per unit prices.

00:45:07.931 --> 00:45:10.500
You have the solar generation,

00:45:10.501 --> 00:45:13.080
which is like we don't
have much control over it.

00:45:13.081 --> 00:45:15.480
This generation is happening
depending upon your weather,

00:45:15.481 --> 00:45:16.510
you can just predict

00:45:16.511 --> 00:45:18.435
degeneration how much
it can be there.

00:45:18.436 --> 00:45:20.780
You have a battery. So
the control that you have

00:45:20.781 --> 00:45:24.110
is how much battery that you
can charge and discharge.

00:45:24.111 --> 00:45:27.420
Again, the problem at

00:45:27.421 --> 00:45:28.990
the small scale is

00:45:28.991 --> 00:45:31.770
being handled at multiple
different stages as well.

00:45:31.771 --> 00:45:32.800
So, there are something which is

00:45:32.801 --> 00:45:34.350
called as Virtual power plants,

00:45:34.351 --> 00:45:36.240
where you have
multiple different types

00:45:36.241 --> 00:45:38.260
of power generation equipment.

00:45:38.261 --> 00:45:41.030
It could be wind, solar, diesel

00:45:41.031 --> 00:45:42.900
generators, solar batteries.

00:45:42.901 --> 00:45:45.985
So, how can you activate?

00:45:45.986 --> 00:45:48.615
So, basically, virtually,
they create a power plant,

00:45:48.616 --> 00:45:51.155
and let's say if you have
the requirement that

00:45:51.156 --> 00:45:52.630
this Virtual power
plant need to be

00:45:52.631 --> 00:45:55.685
supplying a particular amount
of demand during time,

00:45:55.686 --> 00:45:58.850
how can you choose
the right set of

00:45:58.851 --> 00:46:01.080
generation at right time so that

00:46:01.081 --> 00:46:03.440
the overall cost of
generation is minimized?

00:46:03.441 --> 00:46:06.215
So, starting from
the individual household level

00:46:06.216 --> 00:46:08.425
to the large-scale generation,

00:46:08.426 --> 00:46:09.810
the same problem is being

00:46:09.811 --> 00:46:11.490
tackled at multiple
different levels.

00:46:11.491 --> 00:46:19.020
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:46:19.021 --> 00:46:21.515
&gt;&gt; So, in earlier two problems,

00:46:21.516 --> 00:46:25.185
we're still running with
the conventional sources

00:46:25.186 --> 00:46:26.715
but tackling to
the power shortage

00:46:26.716 --> 00:46:28.380
and electricity wastage.

00:46:28.381 --> 00:46:30.510
Here, it's mainly we are taking

00:46:30.511 --> 00:46:33.110
into account other
non-conventional integration,

00:46:33.111 --> 00:46:35.770
and that's happening at
multiple different levels.

00:46:35.771 --> 00:46:37.645
So, you can choose any subside or

00:46:37.646 --> 00:46:40.005
of power generators
which are available.

00:46:40.006 --> 00:46:41.270
Each of them would have

00:46:41.271 --> 00:46:43.655
different pricing for
per unit generation,

00:46:43.656 --> 00:46:46.040
and they have different
availability times

00:46:46.041 --> 00:46:47.245
and different constraints.

00:46:47.246 --> 00:46:49.350
So, taking all these
constraints into account,

00:46:49.351 --> 00:46:51.130
how can you fulfill
your demand in

00:46:51.131 --> 00:46:54.410
real time while minimizing
the total cost of generation?

00:46:54.411 --> 00:46:56.050
So, that's happening
at individual

00:46:56.051 --> 00:46:58.675
household industry level
or the utility level.

00:46:58.676 --> 00:47:00.490
The interesting part here is you

00:47:00.491 --> 00:47:01.980
need to make the decisions of

00:47:01.981 --> 00:47:05.565
which generations to switch
on or off in real time.

00:47:05.566 --> 00:47:10.919
So, this requires
very much computations

00:47:10.920 --> 00:47:14.565
which are as close to
the generators as possible.

00:47:14.566 --> 00:47:17.100
So, if you are let's
say collecting

00:47:17.101 --> 00:47:19.585
data from wind turbines
and solar together,

00:47:19.586 --> 00:47:21.680
obviously, you need to
collect both data and make

00:47:21.681 --> 00:47:24.020
the decisions on the cloud.

00:47:24.021 --> 00:47:25.140
But let's say if you're doing

00:47:25.141 --> 00:47:26.730
these decisions for the house,

00:47:26.731 --> 00:47:28.540
then it's good to
make the decision at

00:47:28.541 --> 00:47:30.860
individual house
level rather than

00:47:30.861 --> 00:47:33.150
collecting data at the cloud
and making the decision

00:47:33.151 --> 00:47:36.560
because of the latency
and network issues.

00:47:39.180 --> 00:47:42.250
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] what's
the cost of each liters

00:47:42.251 --> 00:47:45.335
that you need documents of it?

00:47:45.336 --> 00:47:48.275
&gt;&gt; So, basically, it's called
levelized cost of energy,

00:47:48.276 --> 00:47:51.315
which takes into account
additional costs of

00:47:51.316 --> 00:47:53.180
maintenance and all other things

00:47:53.181 --> 00:47:55.090
over the time of whatever period.

00:47:55.091 --> 00:47:56.800
So, now, if let say
we are taking into

00:47:56.801 --> 00:47:59.325
account batteries
levelized cost of energy,

00:47:59.326 --> 00:48:02.450
you need to take into account
how much battery life is.

00:48:02.451 --> 00:48:04.620
So, whatever garbages
that you are putting in,

00:48:04.621 --> 00:48:07.470
you would be expensing it
over four year's time.

00:48:07.471 --> 00:48:09.080
Depending upon how many

00:48:09.081 --> 00:48:11.180
charge and discharge
cycles you make,

00:48:11.181 --> 00:48:13.400
the battery life is
going to go down.

00:48:13.401 --> 00:48:15.800
So, that is another constraint on

00:48:15.801 --> 00:48:17.010
when you decide how much to

00:48:17.011 --> 00:48:18.490
charge and discharge
the batteries,

00:48:18.491 --> 00:48:21.510
that how many
charging-discharging cycles

00:48:21.511 --> 00:48:23.330
that you are taking into account.

00:48:23.331 --> 00:48:24.860
Each of these resources

00:48:24.861 --> 00:48:26.600
have similar kinds
of constraints.

00:48:26.601 --> 00:48:29.210
So, there are some times

00:48:29.211 --> 00:48:31.700
when wind turbine if
the wind is too low,

00:48:31.701 --> 00:48:34.245
it would still
generate energy but it

00:48:34.246 --> 00:48:36.680
would run very sub-optimally
during those times.

00:48:36.681 --> 00:48:38.450
So, your levelized cost of

00:48:38.451 --> 00:48:41.060
energy during those
times would be

00:48:41.061 --> 00:48:43.160
higher so it might
be better of to run

00:48:43.161 --> 00:48:46.890
some other generator rather
than running it on low.

00:48:47.740 --> 00:48:50.830
So, here, basically
the problem is

00:48:50.831 --> 00:48:53.515
our forecasting generation
from each of these assets.

00:48:53.516 --> 00:48:57.775
So, generally, now even
utilities in India have started

00:48:57.776 --> 00:49:00.420
posing every plant which

00:49:00.421 --> 00:49:02.220
is more than five
megawatt capacity.

00:49:02.221 --> 00:49:03.820
They need to provide one day in

00:49:03.821 --> 00:49:05.550
advance that how much generation

00:49:05.551 --> 00:49:07.185
would be happening from the plant

00:49:07.186 --> 00:49:09.215
at every 15 minutes time slot.

00:49:09.216 --> 00:49:11.735
Then there would be
revisions during their day.

00:49:11.736 --> 00:49:13.640
Similarly for small houses,

00:49:13.641 --> 00:49:14.970
if you are running it for

00:49:14.971 --> 00:49:17.575
the individual small house
or a commercial entity,

00:49:17.576 --> 00:49:19.030
we need to know
how much generation is

00:49:19.031 --> 00:49:22.285
expected from the sources
in real time,

00:49:22.286 --> 00:49:24.450
a day in advance
so that you can do

00:49:24.451 --> 00:49:26.370
the optimization and
if there are changes,

00:49:26.371 --> 00:49:28.800
you need to be able to
adjust to those changes.

00:49:28.801 --> 00:49:31.515
The second is similar to
the generation forecasting,

00:49:31.516 --> 00:49:33.075
you need to do
the load forecasting.

00:49:33.076 --> 00:49:34.590
So, how much demand
is coming from

00:49:34.591 --> 00:49:37.160
your residential or
commercial place

00:49:37.161 --> 00:49:40.345
and how would you be able
to cater to that demand.

00:49:40.346 --> 00:49:42.540
Next is optimization problem to

00:49:42.541 --> 00:49:44.700
comply with the policy. So, yeah.

00:49:44.701 --> 00:49:55.170
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] ?

00:49:55.171 --> 00:49:58.375
&gt;&gt; So, it depends upon how
dividing is done inside house,

00:49:58.376 --> 00:50:00.740
but that's something
which is possible.

00:50:00.741 --> 00:50:04.650
So, because of cost
constraints on something,

00:50:04.651 --> 00:50:07.210
generally, some people do that,

00:50:07.970 --> 00:50:10.830
let's say solar
power is supplied to

00:50:10.831 --> 00:50:12.800
only few appliances and

00:50:12.801 --> 00:50:15.045
grid is supplied to
all the houses kind of a thing,

00:50:15.046 --> 00:50:17.610
but the combination and
mix is possible depending

00:50:17.611 --> 00:50:21.130
upon the wiring that
you do of your house.

00:50:21.131 --> 00:50:23.470
Again, depending upon three-phase

00:50:23.471 --> 00:50:25.800
versus one-phase loads and

00:50:25.801 --> 00:50:28.315
other things there
are few constraints.

00:50:28.316 --> 00:50:31.530
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:50:31.531 --> 00:50:33.870
depend on the time of device?

00:50:33.871 --> 00:50:36.090
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, there are
two types of that.

00:50:36.091 --> 00:50:37.935
So, there is one is

00:50:37.936 --> 00:50:39.630
the consumption tariff where

00:50:39.631 --> 00:50:40.970
the consumer is charged depending

00:50:40.971 --> 00:50:43.540
upon how much amount of

00:50:43.541 --> 00:50:46.430
energy user is consuming
at specific time of day.

00:50:46.431 --> 00:50:47.990
Then there is other type of

00:50:47.991 --> 00:50:49.540
tariff which is called
feed-in tariff,

00:50:49.541 --> 00:50:51.180
so depending upon
how much amount of

00:50:51.181 --> 00:50:53.240
energy you are injecting
into the grid.

00:50:53.241 --> 00:50:55.490
Sometimes it's constant
throughout the day,

00:50:55.491 --> 00:50:58.310
sometimes there is
a time of use as well.

00:50:59.270 --> 00:51:01.670
So, this is the other problem

00:51:01.671 --> 00:51:04.480
where depending upon the pricing

00:51:04.481 --> 00:51:06.180
and the policies which
take into account

00:51:06.181 --> 00:51:07.310
the additional constraints of

00:51:07.311 --> 00:51:08.690
let say levelized
cost of charging,

00:51:08.691 --> 00:51:10.590
discharging batteries,
and battery life,

00:51:10.591 --> 00:51:14.290
the optimization needs
to be done on the grid.

00:51:14.291 --> 00:51:16.835
So, this is a problem which
we are working on right now.

00:51:16.836 --> 00:51:19.905
So, right now, we do
the head forecasting

00:51:19.906 --> 00:51:24.270
and push the results
to the box or Edge.

00:51:24.271 --> 00:51:27.080
Then they do small
optimizational,

00:51:27.081 --> 00:51:29.220
simple optimization if
there are any changes.

00:51:29.221 --> 00:51:31.300
So, we are basically working on

00:51:31.301 --> 00:51:34.255
how we can do the revisions
on the box itself,

00:51:34.256 --> 00:51:36.320
because if there is
network disconnectivity

00:51:36.321 --> 00:51:38.040
or other issues and how we can

00:51:38.041 --> 00:51:40.140
basically provide
the optimization

00:51:40.141 --> 00:51:41.935
and mode of this matter
on the Edge.

00:51:41.936 --> 00:51:46.450
So, there are
penalties associated

00:51:46.451 --> 00:51:48.845
if your generation is not correct

00:51:48.846 --> 00:51:51.290
beyond 15 percent for each
of the 15 minutes point.

00:51:51.291 --> 00:51:53.230
So, these are the policies
in India right now,

00:51:53.231 --> 00:51:56.240
and we need to be able
to cater to those.

00:51:56.241 --> 00:51:59.260
So, another problem which is
not on the Edge but which we

00:51:59.261 --> 00:52:00.450
are trying right now is this is

00:52:00.451 --> 00:52:01.885
the Microsoft Research Building.

00:52:01.886 --> 00:52:03.690
So, if we want to have

00:52:03.691 --> 00:52:06.195
larger adoption of
renewable energy resources,

00:52:06.196 --> 00:52:08.460
we need to be able to
identify the user,

00:52:08.461 --> 00:52:09.830
need to be able to identify how

00:52:09.831 --> 00:52:11.510
much is the potential of solar on

00:52:11.511 --> 00:52:13.220
my particular roof and

00:52:13.221 --> 00:52:15.495
how much amount of energy
that can be generated.

00:52:15.496 --> 00:52:17.820
So, basically, this identifies

00:52:17.821 --> 00:52:21.380
the area of the roof and
classification of the roof.

00:52:21.381 --> 00:52:22.770
So, depending upon the dates,

00:52:22.771 --> 00:52:24.830
the south-north roof or it's

00:52:24.831 --> 00:52:26.935
the east-west roof
or it's a flat roof,

00:52:26.936 --> 00:52:29.180
the capacity of solar
that you can put

00:52:29.181 --> 00:52:31.860
on in then and its all
our potential word body.

00:52:31.861 --> 00:52:33.420
So, we are doing some work on

00:52:33.421 --> 00:52:35.260
the image optimization on there,

00:52:35.261 --> 00:52:37.820
and there is already
some open source work on

00:52:37.821 --> 00:52:40.575
that core loop and solar map
if somebody is interested.

00:52:40.576 --> 00:52:42.810
That was mainly for
the French datasets.

00:52:42.811 --> 00:52:45.700
We are looking at
Indian datasets for this.

00:52:45.701 --> 00:52:47.970
Finally, the
efficient operations.

00:52:47.971 --> 00:52:49.205
So, this is mainly about

00:52:49.206 --> 00:52:51.010
the predictive maintenance
and root-cause.

00:52:51.011 --> 00:52:52.450
So, since we collect data from

00:52:52.451 --> 00:52:54.460
various appliances in solar,

00:52:54.461 --> 00:52:56.420
there are different types of

00:52:56.421 --> 00:52:59.320
failures which are possible
across various sensors,

00:52:59.321 --> 00:53:01.140
including weather
sensors, inverters,

00:53:01.141 --> 00:53:02.535
MPPTs, and other things.

00:53:02.536 --> 00:53:05.160
So, we tried to identify
proactively and

00:53:05.161 --> 00:53:08.965
reactively those faults
and alert the users.

00:53:08.966 --> 00:53:11.140
This is the cycle for data

00:53:11.141 --> 00:53:13.620
collected from
the IoT platform right now,

00:53:13.621 --> 00:53:17.265
and this is how the Real-Time
Portfolio View looks like.

00:53:17.266 --> 00:53:19.680
So, there is something
that we are doing

00:53:19.681 --> 00:53:21.400
like online offline module,

00:53:21.401 --> 00:53:24.220
where the central user wants
to look at the entire data.

00:53:24.221 --> 00:53:26.010
So, if I'm the independent
power producer

00:53:26.011 --> 00:53:27.960
who has 25 plants across India,

00:53:27.961 --> 00:53:30.380
I would want to see
in real time how

00:53:30.381 --> 00:53:33.000
it's performing over
my entire set of plants.

00:53:33.001 --> 00:53:34.660
But many are times
this large plants

00:53:34.661 --> 00:53:35.970
are at remote locations.

00:53:35.971 --> 00:53:37.510
So, there are always

00:53:37.511 --> 00:53:39.765
problems of network connectivity
and other things.

00:53:39.766 --> 00:53:42.500
So, if the entire model is
sitting only on the cloud,

00:53:42.501 --> 00:53:44.920
the site engineers who
are siting at the ground,

00:53:44.921 --> 00:53:46.830
they are not able to access
this system as well.

00:53:46.831 --> 00:53:49.115
So, we have something
like offline sync model,

00:53:49.116 --> 00:53:51.160
where it's a subset
of functionalities

00:53:51.161 --> 00:53:55.080
available on the fog
at the plant itself,

00:53:55.081 --> 00:53:57.210
and opportunistically, it swings

00:53:57.211 --> 00:54:00.425
with the cloud datacenters.

00:54:00.426 --> 00:54:03.620
Then identifying
various under-performing assets.

00:54:03.621 --> 00:54:06.180
So, we have shared some data
with [inaudible] team as well,

00:54:06.181 --> 00:54:07.750
so we would like to explore

00:54:07.751 --> 00:54:10.105
some of the techniques
that they have used here.

00:54:10.106 --> 00:54:12.660
So, this is the
cleaning schedules.

00:54:12.661 --> 00:54:15.470
So, cleaning is one of
the important issues in solar,

00:54:15.471 --> 00:54:17.100
which drives the inefficiencies.

00:54:17.101 --> 00:54:19.090
So, without adding
any additional sensors,

00:54:19.091 --> 00:54:21.625
just like looking at
the power consumption traits,

00:54:21.626 --> 00:54:23.310
we are able to identify when is

00:54:23.311 --> 00:54:25.790
the cleaning required and how
much is assaulting losses.

00:54:25.791 --> 00:54:27.510
Again, this one we were doing on

00:54:27.511 --> 00:54:29.420
the cloud right now and
we're trying to push

00:54:29.421 --> 00:54:31.750
it in the Edge as part of

00:54:31.751 --> 00:54:34.915
the workshop experiment
that we're doing.

00:54:34.916 --> 00:54:38.255
The last one is combining
time-series and image data.

00:54:38.256 --> 00:54:40.650
So, one is we are getting
this time-series data of

00:54:40.651 --> 00:54:42.150
power consumption and
other things from

00:54:42.151 --> 00:54:44.115
individual equipments.

00:54:44.116 --> 00:54:46.140
But we're also trying
to get some data from

00:54:46.141 --> 00:54:49.300
either tomographic imaging
or the normal cameras,

00:54:49.301 --> 00:54:51.610
so how we can
combine these two to

00:54:51.611 --> 00:54:54.270
identify the right fault
and decide what type

00:54:54.271 --> 00:54:56.700
of dust it is or what type
of soiling it is and

00:54:56.701 --> 00:54:59.725
activate the auto cleaning
mechanism appropriately.

00:54:59.726 --> 00:55:02.830
So, there are a lot of
interesting, other startups,

00:55:02.831 --> 00:55:05.370
and other corporates
which are working on

00:55:05.371 --> 00:55:07.420
energy problems using

00:55:07.421 --> 00:55:10.540
AI and IoT technologies
across the world.

00:55:10.541 --> 00:55:14.790
We'll be happy if there
are any problems which are

00:55:14.791 --> 00:55:16.470
of your interest and we'll be

00:55:16.471 --> 00:55:19.330
happy to collaborate
with you. Thank you.

